559	676	1	526	\N	Tell me about a time when project requirements changed significantly. How did you adapt?	\N	medium	In a previous project, requirements changed mid-way due to shifting business priorities. I first assessed the impact on existing deliverables and communicated with stakeholders to clarify the new expectations. I reprioritized tasks, updated timelines, and ensured the team was aligned through regular syncs. By staying flexible and maintaining clear communication, we successfully adapted and delivered the updated solution without major delays.	\N	30000	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "behavioral", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-07 10:06:08.66303+00
560	676	2	191	\N	What is container orchestration? Compare Docker Swarm, Kubernetes, and when to use each.	\N	easy	Container orchestration is the automated management of containerized applications, including deployment, scaling, and networking. Docker Swarm is simple and easy to set up but offers limited flexibility. Kubernetes is more complex but provides advanced features like auto-scaling, self-healing, and extensive ecosystem support. Docker Swarm is suitable for smaller setups or simple deployments, while Kubernetes is preferred for large-scale, production-grade systems requiring high availability and flexibility.	\N	30000	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-07 10:06:21.682209+00
561	676	3	178	\N	Explain process management in Linux. How do you use ps, top, htop, kill, systemctl?	\N	medium	Process management in Linux involves monitoring and controlling running processes. ps provides a snapshot of current processes, while top and htop offer real-time monitoring. kill is used to terminate processes using signals, and systemctl manages system services in systems using systemd. These tools help in diagnosing performance issues, managing resources, and controlling system services effectively.	\N	30000	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-07 10:06:34.907887+00
562	676	4	187	\N	How do you write an optimized Dockerfile? Explain layer caching, multi-stage builds, and security best practices.	\N	medium	An optimized Dockerfile minimizes image size and build time. Layer caching is used by ordering instructions so that frequently changing layers come last. Multi-stage builds allow separating build dependencies from the final image, reducing size. Security best practices include using minimal base images, avoiding root user, and scanning images for vulnerabilities.	\N	30000	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-07 10:06:49.995658+00
563	676	5	184	\N	Explain process substitution, command substitution, and here documents in bash. Provide practical examples.	\N	medium	Process substitution allows using the output of a command as a file input, e.g., diff <(ls dir1) <(ls dir2). Command substitution replaces a command with its output using $(command). Here documents allow multi-line input using <<EOF blocks. These features improve scripting flexibility and readability.	\N	30000	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-07 10:07:04.483551+00
564	676	6	176	\N	Explain the Linux file system hierarchy. What are the purposes of /etc, /var, /usr, /home, /tmp?	\N	easy	The Linux filesystem hierarchy organizes files into directories with specific purposes. /etc contains configuration files, /var stores variable data like logs, /usr holds user programs and libraries, /home contains user directories, and /tmp is used for temporary files.	\N	30000	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-07 10:07:17.839063+00
