583	680	1	532	\N	Please give me a concise self-introduction: your background, your most relevant technical experience, and one recent project you are proud of.	\N	easy	I am a software engineer with experience in building scalable backend systems using microservices architecture. I have worked extensively with technologies like Java, Spring Boot, Kafka, and distributed systems. Recently, I built an observability framework integrating Prometheus metrics, OpenTelemetry tracing, and centralized logging, which improved system debugging and reduced incident resolution time significantly.	\N	15573	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "self_introduction", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-17 08:56:23.863938+00
584	680	2	524	\N	Give an example of how you mentored or helped a junior team member grow.	\N	medium	I mentored a junior developer by guiding them through backend service design and code reviews. Initially, they struggled with writing scalable APIs, so I helped them understand concepts like modular design, error handling, and performance optimization. Over time, they became confident enough to independently deliver features and even contributed improvements to our codebase.	\N	10314	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "behavioral", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-17 08:56:34.233466+00
585	680	3	476	\N	What are microservices and how do they differ from monolithic architecture	\N	easy	Microservices architecture structures an application as a collection of loosely coupled services, each responsible for a specific business capability, whereas a monolithic architecture is a single unified codebase. Microservices allow independent deployment, scalability, and fault isolation, while monoliths are simpler to develop initially but become hard to scale and maintain as the system grows.	\N	10455	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-17 08:56:44.774674+00
586	680	4	460	\N	Your social media application has grown from 100K to 10M users and the database is becoming a bottleneck with slow queries and frequent timeouts. Discuss strategies for scaling including read replicas, caching layers, database sharding approaches, query optimization, and handling eventual consistency	\N	hard	To scale the database, I would first introduce read replicas to distribute read traffic and reduce load on the primary database. Then, I would add a caching layer like Redis to minimize repeated database hits for frequently accessed data. For horizontal scaling, I would implement sharding based on user or data domain to distribute data across multiple nodes. Query optimization through indexing and avoiding N+1 queries is critical. Eventual consistency can be handled using asynchronous processing and message queues, ensuring the system remains available while maintaining acceptable consistency guarantees.	\N	26383	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-17 08:57:11.248249+00
587	680	5	478	\N	Compare different communication patterns in microservices: synchronous HTTP, asynchronous messaging, and event-driven	\N	medium	Synchronous HTTP communication is simple and provides immediate responses but creates tight coupling and latency issues. Asynchronous messaging using queues like Kafka or RabbitMQ decouples services and improves reliability but adds complexity. Event-driven architecture allows services to react to events without direct dependencies, improving scalability and flexibility, but requires careful design to handle ordering, retries, and consistency.	\N	13656	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-17 08:57:24.953353+00
588	680	6	455	\N	You are tasked with redesigning a monolithic e-commerce application that is struggling with Black Friday traffic spikes and slow deployment cycles. Discuss your approach to identifying bottlenecks, deciding what to extract first, handling data consistency during migration, and ensuring zero downtime during the transition	\N	hard	I would start by identifying bottlenecks using metrics, logs, and tracing to understand performance issues. Then, I would extract loosely coupled modules such as user management or order processing into independent services using the strangler pattern. Data consistency during migration can be handled using techniques like dual writes, change data capture, or eventual consistency models. To ensure zero downtime, I would use gradual traffic shifting, feature flags, and backward-compatible APIs while continuously monitoring system health.	\N	23559	\N	{"final_intent": null, "section_name": "topics_assessment", "question_type": "text", "intent_sequence": [], "response_language": null, "audio_recording_id": null, "code_submission_id": null, "clarification_count": 0, "final_intent_confidence": null, "clarification_exchange_ids": [], "clarification_limit_exceeded": false}	2026-04-17 08:57:48.549789+00
